<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/6th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Battle</p><h1 class="enc-title">Battle of Mons Lactarius</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">552 or 553</span> · <a href="/centuries/6th-century">6th century</a> · Part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gothic-war-535-554">Gothic War (535–554)</a> · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Battle of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mons">Mons</a> Lactarius was fought near Mount Vesuvius in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/campania">Campania</a> in 552 or 553 between the Byzantine army of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/narses">Narses</a> and the last Ostrogothic king, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/teia">Teia</a>. After a stand-off of about two months the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/goths">Goths</a> attacked, and Teia was killed in the fighting. The defeat marked the end of the Ostrogothic Kingdom as an organised power in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/battle-of-mons-lactarius.jpg" alt="Battle of Mons Lactarius" width="320" height="351" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: A.Cython · CC BY 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Gothic_War_army_movements_second_phase.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>In 540 the Gothic capital Ravenna surrendered to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belisarius">Belisarius</a>, bringing most of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> under Byzantine control. After he was recalled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a>, corruption among Byzantine commanders and harsh taxation revived Gothic resistance. The new king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/totila">Totila</a> gained support by treating Italian civilians more fairly than some Byzantine officials did, and after nearly a decade he had recaptured much of Italy. The emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/justinian-i">Justinian I</a> sent his chamberlain <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/narses">Narses</a>, who assembled an army at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amfissa">Salona</a> that included <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-lombards">Lombards</a>, Heruls and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gepids">Gepids</a>. Totila was mortally wounded at Taginae and his army scattered. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/goths">Goths</a> gathered at Ticinum and chose <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/teia">Teia</a> as king; the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frankish-kingdom">Franks</a> refused his plea for an alliance, preferring to let the Goths and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantines</a> wear each other down.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/narses">Narses</a> took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/narni">Narni</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spoleto">Spoleto</a> and recaptured <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> in July, with its keys sent to Justinian. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/goths">Goths</a> then executed their hostages, among them senators with their families and 300 children <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/totila">Totila</a> had taken from leading families, and carried out widespread killings of the Italian population. Narses besieged Cumae, where Totila's war chest was kept and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/teia">Teia</a>'s brother commanded the garrison.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/teia">Teia</a> marched south by long detours that took nearly thirty days. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/narses">Narses</a> intercepted him near Mount Vesuvius, and Teia camped at the foot of the mountain on the left bank of the Sarno, the two armies facing each other across a small stream with a single bridge. The standoff lasted nearly two months, with skirmishes and single combats at the bridge, until Narses brought up his fleet and the Gothic admiral surrendered the Gothic ships, cutting off supply.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/teia">Teia</a> withdrew toward Mount Lactarius and launched a surprise attack on 1 or 30 October. Both armies fought on foot in deep formations, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantines</a> held. Throwing spears were aimed at Teia, whose companions kept supplying him with new shields, until he died. His head was fixed on a stake to discourage the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/goths">Goths</a>, who nevertheless fought on for two more days.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/goths">Goths</a> sent an envoy to ask to leave <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> with their money, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/narses">Narses</a> agreed on strict conditions on the advice of John. The survivors swore to leave Italy, but a thousand Goths under Indulf broke away, and Aligern held Cumae and Ragnaris Conza della <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/campania">Campania</a>. The Goths failed to choose a new king. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frankish-kingdom">Franks</a> under the Alamannic dukes Lothar and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/butilin">Buccelin</a> entered Italy with 75,000 men, and in October 554 Narses defeated them at the Battle of the Volturnus. Campsa fell in 555 and Verona in 561.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Battle of Mons Lactarius” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Totila was killed at Busta Gallorum in 552, and Teia at <b>Mons Lactarius</b> in 552 or 553.</blockquote><a href="/state/ostrogothic-kingdom">Ostrogothic Kingdom</a></li></ul></section>
